From b704ffd663177599f034592b41199f3c9ed407e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ferdinand Vanmaele <info@fvanmaele.de>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:30:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [clangd] Compile headers with the command of an includer from
 the index

A header has no compile command of its own; clangd borrows one from an open
file that includes it once that file's preamble is built (TUScheduler's
HeaderIncluderCache), and otherwise uses a command the compilation database
guesses from file names. The choice is made when the header is opened and
kept until it changes. With --navigation-from-index an #include jump opens
the header before the includer is parsed, and a header opened first never
had an includer: on a project with a vendored library the guess came from
that library's source and the header showed "file not found" errors.

The background index knows an includer of every header it has stored: the
loader walks each translation unit's include graph and records the TU for
every shard it reaches (LoadedShard::DependentTU), and indexing in this
session sees the same graph. BackgroundIndex::includerOf returns that TU,
waiting (bounded) while stored shards are still loading. With the hidden
flag --header-flags-from-index the TUScheduler's compilation database asks
it for files without a reliable command and transfers the includer's
command; HeaderIncluderCache still takes precedence. Headers no source file
includes keep the guessed command (logged).

Synthetic project with a vendored fmt library, second session: a header
reached by an #include jump, and the same header opened first, 2 errors
("Kokkos_Macros.hpp file not found", command inferred from
fmt/src/format.cc) -> 0 errors (inferred from src/main.cc).
ClangdTests 1413/1413 (new: BackgroundIndexTest.IncluderOfHeaders).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
